# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 3.0
%modern
# allow the weak reference operator (:=)
%allow-weak-references

# 3.12: DataProvider::getModuleHostHooks()
%requires(reexport) DataProvider >= 3.12
%requires(reexport) TypeScriptProxy
%requires ConnectionProvider
%requires ProviderIndexUtil
%requires RestClient
%requires RestClientIo
%requires Swagger
%requires OpenApi3
%requires RestSchemaDataProvider
%requires FileLocationHandler
%requires FsUtil
%requires Util
%requires Logger
%requires AsyncSocketIo
%requires GoogleDataProvider

%requires json
%requires process
%requires v8
%requires yaml
%requires uuid

module TypeScriptActionInterface {
    version = "1.1";
    desc = "User module providing an API for Qore DataProvider app actions in TypeScript";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        # the hooks the host set before the module was loaded are validated and applied first, before anything is
        # registered: a hook that is misspelled or not callable leaves no factory, app or hook behind
        # (qoretechnologies/qore#6845)
        TenantActionRunner::applyHostHooks();

        # Publish a lightweight exact identity inventory for qualified index discovery.
        DataProviderActionCatalog::registerDiscoveryInventoryProvider(
            TypeScriptActionInterface::DiscoveryInventoryProviderName,
            \TypeScriptActionInterface::getDiscoveryInventory());

        # Register the module's initializer
        DataProvider::registerStaticInitializer(\TypeScriptActionInterface::init());

        # Register the data provider factory
        DataProvider::registerFactory(new TypeScriptActionDataProviderFactory());

        # initialize apps with deferred action initialization
        TypeScriptActionInterfacePriv::init();

        # the runner of tenants' calls: its trusted build capability is created now, before any tenant code runs
        TenantActionRunner::init();
    };
    # every tenant's event source is stopped and its program freed when the module is unloaded
    del = sub () {
        TenantActionRunner::shutdown();
    };
    # THREAD_CONTROL: the module starts its own background tasks
    # PROCESS_CONTROL: its init registers data provider factory (process-wide registrations)
    # (a Program restricted like a Qorus interface can load the module only when it declares them; Qore #5803)
    # the public static methods of TypeScriptActionInterface::TypeScriptActionInterface with a process-wide effect (the
    # registrations of apps, actions and child providers, the logger, the server) declare PROCESS_CONTROL themselves
    # ([dom=PROCESS_CONTROL]), so that a restricted caller is refused at the API and cannot use the module's declaration
    domains = "THREAD_CONTROL,PROCESS_CONTROL";
}

%ifdef QoreDebug
public sub ASSERT(*softbool b) {
    if (!b) {
        throw "ASSERTION-FAILED", "assertion failed";
    }
}
%else
public sub ASSERT(*softbool b) {}
%endif

/** @mainpage TypeScriptActionInterface Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section typescriptactioninterfaceintro TypeScriptActionInterface Module Introduction

    @anchor TypeScriptActionInterfaceintro

    The %TypeScriptActionInterface module provides a @ref dataproviderintro "data provider" and app action API for
    TypeScript code.

    @section typescriptactioninterface_reference_data Reference Data Failures and Retries

    Reference data lookups, such as channel and user choices in an action form, cache successful results for ten
    minutes. Concurrent callers share an in-progress lookup. If the lookup fails, all callers using that entry
    receive the error, and the next lookup can retry immediately. An empty successful result remains cacheable.

    TypeScript app lookup helpers propagate failed API requests instead of returning empty choices, partial
    pages, default values, or incomplete dynamic schemas. The shared API client also rejects malformed
    collections and invalid pagination continuations. Bounded app-specific scans report deadline expiration
    as an error. Record operations requiring those reads abort before their write phase if selection or field
    resolution fails. Existing result caps and placeholders for unselected prerequisite options still apply.

    Slack channel, archived-channel and user choices also have a per-connection sixty-second cache in the
    TypeScript catalogue. Slack pagination rejects transport errors, HTTP errors, API errors and incomplete scans
    that exceed the timeout; it never returns a failed scan as an empty or partial successful result. Pages with no
    items still advance when Slack supplies a continuation cursor. The configured result limit still applies.

    Slack read requests retry HTTP 429 responses up to three times, using Slack's \c Retry-After delay. Each scan
    shares a sixty-second budget by default across requests, page delays and retries. If the requested retry delay
    does not fit in the remaining budget, the rate-limit error is returned without making an early retry.

    @par Example
    If loading a Slack channel selector fails with \c missing_scope, correct the connection's permissions and
    request its choices again. The failed lookup does not prevent a fresh request for the next ten minutes.

    @section typescriptactioninterfacecatalogs Schema-Backed Apps and Connections

    Install matching versions of the Qore module and the JavaScript distribution. Catalogue protocol 2
    detects incompatible installations before registration. Apps without their required schemas report
    <tt>APP-SCHEMAS-UNAVAILABLE</tt>. Their identity and connection scheme remain available.
    The <tt>qore-app-schemas</tt> tool manages the schemas used by the installed distribution.

    Generated REST connections publish localized option labels and allowed values. An action field can
    use <tt>metadata.create_action</tt> to identify an action that creates one of its values.
    REST clients retain their owning connection when copied. Use
    <tt>TypeScriptAppRestClient::introspectOAuth2Token()</tt> when the app supports token introspection.

    @section typescriptactioninterface_time_limits Time Limits

    Each call of an app's or action's function, such as an action's API function, a record operation, or a webhook
    registration, has a time limit. A call that has not completed when its limit is reached fails with
    \c JAVASCRIPT-ASYNC-ERROR ("Request timed out after ... ms"). The limit is 30 minutes by default, so long API
    and AI actions can complete. Change it for all apps with
    @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TypeScriptActionInterface::setCallTimeout()
    "TypeScriptActionInterface::setCallTimeout()", or by setting the \c QORE_TYPESCRIPT_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECS
    environment variable to a number of seconds before the module is loaded. An app or action can set its own
    limit in seconds with \c io_timeout_secs. Event functions have no limit; they run until their events are
    stopped.

    When apps run in \c ts-proxy child processes, each child applies the limit that is set when it is started. A
    new child has 60 seconds to start; a child that has not started by then is terminated, and the action fails
    with \c CHILD-ERROR. Change this with @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::JavaScriptProgramProxy::setStartupTimeout()
    "JavaScriptProgramProxy::setStartupTimeout()", or by setting the \c QORE_TYPESCRIPT_PROXY_STARTUP_TIMEOUT_SECS
    environment variable to a number of seconds.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# allow long AI actions up to one hour, and child processes two minutes to start on a busy host
TypeScriptActionInterface::setCallTimeout(1h);
JavaScriptProgramProxy::setStartupTimeout(2m);
    @endcode

    @anchor TypeScriptActionInterface_relnotes

    @subpage typescriptactioninterfacereleasenotes

    @section typescriptactioninterface_tenants Running Actions for Tenants

    @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner "TenantActionRunner" runs the API and record actions of the
    module's apps for callers that a sandbox governs (tenants), with plain data only (qoretechnologies/qore#6845):
    - the caller gives a @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TsTenantContext "TsTenantContext": the tenant (the isolation
      key of the call; required), its own resolved connection options, and the action's options and time limit; it
      never gives a Program, a connection or a provider object
    - the app runs in a restricted program of the tenant's own (see the v8 module's restricted programs), which the
      module builds and seals in a trusted build before the program runs anything for the tenant: the program
      reaches no file, process or raw socket, and no program, global or cache is shared between tenants
    - the program reaches the network only through the host bridge, which makes each request under the calling
      code's own sandbox, so that its network policy applies to every address that a request connects to, after
      resolution and for each redirect; the host can refuse more with
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::setEgressPolicy() "setEgressPolicy()" and meter the bytes
      with @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::setEgressMeter() "setEgressMeter()"
    - a call has a time limit, a heap cap and a limit of concurrent calls per tenant
      (@ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::setTenantLimits() "setTenantLimits()")
    - the host gives the runner its hooks and limits before anything loads the module, with
      <tt>DataProvider::setModuleHostHooks("TypeScriptActionInterface", ...)</tt> (keys \c tenant_context_resolver,
      \c connection_update_handler, \c egress_policy, \c egress_meter, \c trusted_call_sandbox and
      \c tenant_limits, a plain hash of
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TsTenantLimits "TsTenantLimits"): the module's initialization applies them, so
      that a module loaded lazily, in whichever thread first asks for an app, has them too; an unknown key, a value of
      the wrong type or a limit that is not greater than 0 fails the initialization.  The host sets them at its
      startup even if it gives none (<tt>{}</tt>): the module's entry of the registry can be set once, and other
      trusted code that set it first would give hooks the host did not choose.  Any trusted code can read the
      registry's hooks (<tt>DataProvider::getModuleHostHooks()</tt>)
    - an app that needs a builtin of Node.js that tenants do not get is refused for tenants
      (\c TS-TENANT-APP-UNSUPPORTED), and
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::getActionInfo() "getActionInfo()" reports it unavailable
    - the apps of the master catalogue and of the scripts of \c QORE_TYPESCRIPT_ACTION_SCRIPTS run for tenants with no
      call of the host: the module records the catalogue that registered each app
    - sandboxed code that uses the module's data providers and connections directly (actions, records, allowed
      values, dependent options, dynamic types, the OAuth hooks of a connection) reaches an app's code only through
      the runner: every call of the module into an app's code for sandboxed code is made by
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::dispatch() "dispatch()", in the tenant's own program, with
      the tenant context that the host's resolver returns for the connection
      (@ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::setTenantContextResolver() "setTenantContextResolver()"),
      or refused if there is none; the pools and proxies of the full runtime refuse sandboxed code, and a connection
      update in a response goes to the host's handler
      (@ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::setConnectionUpdateHandler()
      "setConnectionUpdateHandler()")
    - the event actions of the apps run for tenants as event sources whose events the host pulls:
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::startEvents() "startEvents()" runs the app's event function in
      a restricted program of the tenant's own until
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::stopEvents() "stopEvents()" ends it, whether or not it
      cooperates (its program is terminated, and a request that it waits in is cancelled);
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::pollEvents() "pollEvents()" returns its queued events and
      its checkpoint; a subscription belongs to its tenant; a source that queues its limit of events is stopped and
      reported, never dropping events; every source stops when the process ends (an exit hook) or the module is
      unloaded; webhook events and the event data providers are not available to sandboxed code
    - the host can learn up front which apps and actions tenants can run, when it builds its catalogue, with
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::probeApps() "probeApps()": each app is loaded in a
      restricted program, and the module graph of the app and of the packages that it requires is scanned for the
      builtins of Node.js that tenants do not get; one that a function requires (when it runs, after the seal) makes
      the app unavailable if its shared code reaches it, and otherwise each action whose own code reaches it;
      @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner::getActionInfo() "getActionInfo()" reports both
*/

/** @page typescriptactioninterfacereleasenotes TypeScriptActionInterface Release Notes

    @section typescriptactioninterfaceupcoming Upcoming release
    - @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::TenantActionRunner "TenantActionRunner": the API and record actions of the apps
      for tenants that a sandbox governs, in restricted programs of their own, with plain data, the network only through
      the tenant's sandbox, and time, memory and concurrency limits; see @ref typescriptactioninterface_tenants
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/6845">issue 6845</a>); their event actions run as
      event sources whose events the host pulls
    - a call of trusted code for a tenant's connection (a host's REST handler, flow or test engine) also runs in the
      tenant's program: the host's tenant context resolver is asked for the connection of every call, and decides
      from the connection, not from the calling thread
    - the host gives the runner its hooks before the module is loaded, with
      <tt>DataProvider::setModuleHostHooks("TypeScriptActionInterface", ...)</tt>, so that a module loaded lazily has
      them too
    - Action execution supports a shared in-process pool alongside the existing child-process proxy.
      See @ref TypeScriptActionInterface::JavaScriptInProcessPool.
    - Apps can use localized connection metadata, schema-backed REST actions and OAuth token introspection.
      See @ref typescriptactioninterfacecatalogs for the provider and deployment contracts.
    - Child-process proxies recover from a closed command socket, inherit the environment at startup,
      and enforce startup and call deadlines. See @ref typescriptactioninterface_time_limits.
    - Reference-data failures propagate their provider error. Failed authorization and connection errors
      can be handled by callers. See @ref typescriptactioninterface_reference_data.

    @section TypeScriptActionInterface_v1_1 TypeScriptActionInterface v1.1
    - Added support for Google apps.

    @section TypeScriptActionInterface_v1_0 TypeScriptActionInterface v1.0
    - initial release
*/
